A relief valve is a critical component in hydraulic systems, designed to regulate pressure and protect system components from potential damage. It does this by providing a pathway for excess fluid to bypass the system and flow directly to the reservoir when pressure exceeds a predetermined limit.
Relief valves serve two primary functions:
Protecting system components from excessive pressure
, preventing damage to pumps, actuators, and other hydraulic elements.
Assisting flow control valves in regulating actuator speed
, ensuring smooth and controlled operation.
Without a relief valve, a hydraulic system would be at risk of pressure buildup, which could lead to component failure, leaks, or even catastrophic system damage.